 # Ellik Evacuator
 
-The **Ellik bladder evacuator** is a double-chambered manual irrigation-and-aspiration bulb attached to the [resectoscope](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/resectoscope) or [cystoscope](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/rigid-cystoscope) sheath, used to flush and retrieve **bladder contents — chips, clots, and small fragments** — at the conclusion of any transurethral procedure. Invented by **Milo Ellik** at the University of Iowa in the 1930s, the design has remained essentially unchanged because the compression-decompression / gravity-trap mechanism is hard to improve on.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+The **Ellik evacuator** is a hand-operated irrigation-and-aspiration device that connects to a compatible [resectoscope](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/resectoscope) or [cystoscope](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/rigid-cystoscope) sheath. Its established role is removal of resected tissue, small fragments, and mobile clot during endoscopic bladder or prostate procedures. Milo Ellik described a modification of an evacuator in 1937; the original paper remains the appropriate source for the historical design claim.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
 
-## Design
+## Device design and setup
 
-- **Two stacked transparent chambers** (glass or polycarbonate) joined by a one-way internal pathway.
-- **Compressible bulb** between them.
-- **Nozzle** with a tapered fitting that mates to standard resectoscope / cystoscope sheaths.
-- **Upper chamber** acts as the irrigation reservoir; **lower chamber** acts as the gravity-settling trap for retrieved solids.
-- Reusable (glass / autoclavable) or single-use plastic variants.
+Ellik evacuators are sold in more than one configuration, so the instructions for the exact model govern assembly, reuse, sterilization, adapters, and material precautions. For example:
 
-## Mechanism
+- BD describes its current Ellik family as tissue-section evacuators for transurethral prostate resection and bladder biopsy and lists both disposable and reusable products.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
+- The Bard catalog 000451 instructions describe a **single-use**, ethylene-oxide-sterilized device that must not be resterilized, contains natural-rubber latex, connects directly to Richard Wolf and Karl Storz resectoscope sheaths, and requires a separate adapter for ACMI sheaths.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+- That model is filled with the intended solution and all air is displaced before connection. Compression drives fluid through the sheath; release draws fluid and suspended material back into the device. The manufacturer's instructions do not specify a universal suction pressure.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
 
-Manual squeeze-and-release cycle:
+:::caution[Model-specific safety]
+Do not infer that every Ellik is reusable, latex-free, compatible with every sheath, or suitable for resterilization. Check the packaging and instructions for use for the product actually opened.
+:::
 
-1. **Compress** the bulb → fluid is driven into the bladder, agitating chips, clots, and fragments into suspension.
-2. **Release** the bulb → ~ 80–100 mmHg of negative pressure aspirates the suspended material back through the sheath.
-3. The one-way pathway directs returning debris into the **lower chamber**, where it settles by gravity.
-4. Clear supernatant in the upper chamber is reused on the next cycle.
+## Operative use
 
-This allows continuous closed-circuit evacuation without disconnecting the sheath.
+A practical cycle is:
 
-## Reconstructive / Functional Urology Uses
+1. Establish endoscopic access and confirm that the sheath and evacuator are compatible.
+2. Fill the device with the procedure-appropriate sterile irrigant and remove air as directed by the manufacturer.
+3. With the sheath tip safely within the bladder under the operative team's control, compress and release the bulb to mobilize and recover tissue or clot.
+4. Avoid forceful cycling against resistance or suctioning the bladder wall into the sheath.
+5. Reinspect the bladder and operative bed endoscopically; an evacuator does not replace the final visual survey.
 
-The Ellik is on the back table of essentially every transurethral case relevant to WARWIKI:
+The device is useful after tissue-resecting procedures and selected intravesical lithotripsy or clot-evacuation cases. Whether recovered material is submitted to pathology, and how it is handled, follows the operation, pathology protocol, and surgeon's orders rather than a device-wide rule.
 
-- **Simple prostatectomy / BPH surgery — chip evacuation** at the end of [TURP](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/resectoscope), [HoLEP / ThuLEP](/docs/surgical-techniques/bph-male-luts/prostate-enucleation-holep) enucleation, plasmakinetic enucleation, and any LUTS-surgery chip-producing modality. The retrieved tissue is sent for pathologic survey for incidental cancer per AUA / EAU LUTS guidelines.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Post-procedure gross-hematuria clot evacuation** following any transurethral or open bladder procedure, post-radiation cystitis, post-AUS / IPP revision, or post-cystotomy — the dominant non-OR daytime use in the inpatient setting.
-- **Bladder-stone / fragment retrieval** after intravesical lithotripsy at the time of bladder-stone evacuation for outlet-obstruction sequelae, neurogenic-bladder stones, or stones on long-indwelling foreign material (catheter, sling, mesh, AUS cuff erosion).
-- **Foreign-body retrieval** — small fragments after intentional or unintentional foreign-body cystoscopy.
-- **TURBT chip evacuation** is the bladder-cancer use case; out of WARWIKI's primary scope but mentioned because the instrument is identical and the reconstructive trainee will encounter it on combined trays.
+## Limits and escalation
 
-## Technique Pearls
+An Ellik works best for material that can pass through the sheath. Large, organized, adherent, or compartmentalized clot may require continued endoscopic fragmentation, a different evacuation method, or operative escalation.
 
-- **Connect with the sheath nozzle in the bladder, not in the urethra** — premature disconnection during withdrawal sprays chips into the urethra and prostatic fossa.
-- **Steady rhythm beats hard squeezes** — a slow 1-second compress, 1-second release is more effective than rapid hard pumps, which generate cavitation bubbles and rebreak chips.
-- **Empty the lower chamber before it fills** to the level of the internal pathway — overfilling reintroduces sediment into the irrigation stream.
-- **Send the entire lower-chamber contents** to pathology in saline; piecemeal sampling under-represents both volume and incidental cancer detection.
-- **Pre-warm the irrigant** (saline / sterile water as appropriate to the procedure) to reduce intraoperative hypothermia in the long resection case.
+Goel and colleagues reported direct cystoscope- or resectoscope-sheath suction in **15 adults with old, tenacious clot after conventional Ellik or Toomey evacuation had failed**. The series used suction up to 250 mmHg and cleared every bladder, with a mean operative time of 88 minutes; punctate hemorrhagic spots were the reported mucosal finding.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup> This was a small operative salvage series, not evidence for routine wall suction or unsupervised bedside use.
 
-## Limitations and Failure Modes
+Chemical or enzymatic clot-dissolution approaches are also not routine extensions of the instrument. A chymotrypsin/sodium-bicarbonate report and a single-center retrospective hydrogen-peroxide series describe selected refractory cases, but neither establishes a standard regimen.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[6]](#ref6)</sup> The hydrogen-peroxide report included 31 patients and recorded successful evacuation in 27; concentration, dwell, tissue safety, and applicability remain protocol-dependent.<sup>[[6]](#ref6)</sup>
 
-- **Tenacious / organized clot retention** — the Ellik's ~ 80–100 mmHg ceiling is often insufficient. **Direct connection of the cystoscope sheath to centralized wall suction (~ 250 mmHg)** has been described as a salvage technique, successfully evacuating clots in patients who failed traditional Ellik evacuation.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
-- **Adjuncts for refractory clot retention**:
-  - **Chymotrypsin / sodium-bicarbonate intravesical irrigation** — non-surgical enzymatic dissolution.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
-  - **Hydrogen-peroxide bladder irrigation** — simple, effective in case-series data for clot evacuation when mechanical methods fail.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup>
-- **Lost-chip risk** — chips can sequester in bladder diverticula, behind the trigone, or in the prostatic fossa; perform a deliberate sweep of all walls under direct vision before terminating the case.
-- **Glass-bulb breakage** is the historical injury class — modern plastic variants have largely eliminated this; if a glass Ellik is in use, inspect for fracture lines before each compression cycle.
+## Ellik and catheter washout are different workflows
 
-## Ellik vs Alternatives for Bladder Content Evacuation
+| Device | Interface | Best-supported role |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Ellik evacuator** | Compatible endoscopic sheath | Operative recovery of resected tissue, small fragments, and mobile clot |
+| **[Toomey-tip or catheter-tip syringe](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/suction/toomey-syringe)** | Matching catheter or tubing interface | Manual catheter washout under an ordered clinical protocol |
+| **Continuous bladder irrigation** | Three-way catheter and irrigation set | Maintain drainage after selected bleeding procedures; not a substitute for removing established clot |
 
-| Method | Suction range | Best fit | Limitation |
-|---|---|---|---|
-| **Ellik evacuator** | ~ 80–100 mmHg | Routine chip / small-clot / fragment retrieval | Fails on organized clot burden |
-| **Direct sheath-to-wall-suction (~ 250 mmHg)** | ~ 250 mmHg | Salvage for tenacious clot when Ellik fails<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> | Higher mucosal-injury risk if applied to bladder wall |
-| **Toomey syringe (60 mL) hand evacuation** | Variable | Bedside / non-OR clot evacuation through a Foley | Slow; no closed loop |
-| **Enzymatic dissolution (chymotrypsin / NaHCO₃)** | N/A | Non-surgical alternative for refractory clot retention<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup> | Hours to days to act |
-| **H₂O₂ irrigation** | N/A | Simple bedside / OR adjunct for clot dissolution<sup>[[5]](#ref5)</sup> | Case-series evidence only |
