-## Historical Context — Milo Ellik
-
-**Milo Ellik (1905–1992)** was a urologist at the University of Iowa who introduced the evacuator in the 1930s to address the chip-retrieval bottleneck at the end of open and transurethral prostatectomy. The instrument's elegant two-chamber gravity-trap design has survived nearly a century of redesign attempts and remains on the standard back table for every transurethral case in modern urology.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+See also: [Resectoscope](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/resectoscope), [Rigid Cystoscope](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/rigid-cystoscope), and [Three-Way Catheter](/docs/foundations/tools/biomaterials/urinary-catheters/three-way-catheter).
 
